/*
# Dialog

A modal over its context: everything behind it is held back — presses aimed past it go
nowhere and do not close it — and Tab stays inside it. It closes by its buttons, or Esc.
Its content is yours (its children), and an alert is the most common use, so it carries
the alert verbs too: a title, a message, and buttons as **records** (the records door):
`{ id, label, default?, cancel? }`, rendered as themed library Buttons in the platform
arrangement. Open it with a verb — `show()` for a dialog of your own content, `ask`, `notice`,
`error` or `open` for an alert. Focus is saved on open and restored on close, one turn late,
deliberately: an immediate restore would hand the in-flight keystroke back to the opener. It
fades in and out.

Keyboard: Return picks the default button — unless the focused view takes Return
itself: a focused button or control presses itself (Return on Cancel is Cancel), and a
multi-line field types a line break. Escape picks the cancel record when one exists,
else just closes. Every control inside keeps its own keys (Space, the arrows).

The idioms — one verb each, an optional callback receiving the picked id
(the `picked(id)` override remains the declarative seam):

    dlg.notice("Saved", "Your changes were saved.")
    dlg.ask("Save changes?", "Unsaved changes will be lost.", (id) => …)
    dlg.error("Could not connect", "The server refused the connection.")

`ask` is Cancel / No / Yes-default; `error` renders the title in the
theme's error.

**Platform structure is data**: `dialogWidth`, `dialogButtonLayout` = `"end"` (a
right-aligned row — San Francisco, Material) | `"stack"` (the stacked alert —
full-width buttons stacked, default on top) | `"fill"` (WinUI ContentDialog —
equal-width buttons filling the row), `dialogTextAlign` = `"left"` | `"center"`
(the Cupertino centered title), `dialogTitleFontSize` / `dialogBodyFontSize`. Plus
`scrimFill` + `scrimOpacity` (Cupertino barely dims — alerts separate by
shadow; Material's scrim is 32%; Fluent's smoke 30%), the `menuShadow` family,
`error`.

**Declare one and call it.** A `Dialog` is not a view you show and hide around your
content — it is a standing member you open with a verb, so the same instance serves
every prompt in the program.

```declare
App [ width = 520, height = 260, said: string = "",
    dlg: Dialog [ ],
    Button [ x = 20, y = 20, label = "Delete…",
        onClick() { app.dlg.ask("Delete this file?", "This cannot be undone.",
                                (id) => { app.said = id }) }
        ],
    Text [ x = 20, y = 80, text = { app.said == "" ? "—" : "you chose: " + app.said } ]
    ]
```

A dialog of your own:

```declare
App [ width = 520, height = 300,
    rename: Dialog [ title = "Rename", buttons = { [ { id: "cancel", label: "Cancel", cancel: true }, { id: "ok", label: "Rename", default: true } ] },
        TextInput [ width = 100%, accessibilityLabel = "New name" ]
        ],
    Button [ x = 20, y = 20, label = "Rename…", onClick() { app.rename.show() } ]
    ]
```

## picked()
**The declarative delivery seam — override it at the use site.** Receives the picked
button's `id`. Fires after the dialog has left the screen, and *in addition to* any
callback passed to the verb, so you may use either or both.

## ask()
The three-way prompt: Cancel / No / Yes, with Yes as the default. Delivers `"cancel"`,
`"no"` or `"yes"`.

## notice()
A single-OK acknowledgement. Delivers `"ok"`.

## error()
A single-OK acknowledgement with the title in the theme's error colour. Delivers `"ok"`.

## open()
The general form the three idioms are built on: title, message, your own button
records, a tone (`"error"` or `""`), and an optional callback. Reach for it when the
three idioms do not spell your choices — **button records are `{ id, label, default?,
cancel? }`**, and the two flags are what Return and Escape key off.

## pick()
Delivers a button by id: **dismisses first, then delivers**, one painted frame later —
the same contract as `Menu.pick`, so a slow action cannot freeze the dialog on screen.
Guarded against double activation, so a focused default button answering Space *and*
Return is harmless.

## pickDefault()
Picks whichever button record carries `default: true` — Return's action, the Cupertino rule.

## returnTaken()
Whether the focused view takes Return itself — a control inside the dialog (it presses
itself) or a multi-line field (a line break) — so Return does not also pick the default.

## pickCancel()
Picks the `cancel: true` record when there is one, else just closes — Escape's action.
**A dialog with no cancel record still dismisses on Escape**, it simply delivers
nothing.

## title
The heading. Set by the verbs; read it if you are restyling.

## message
The body text under the title. `""` hides the line entirely rather than leaving a gap.

## buttons
The button records currently rendered, in authored order. The `"stack"` arrangement
reorders them for display — default on top, cancel at the bottom — without touching
this.

## tone
`"error"` tints the title with the theme's error colour; `""` is the ordinary tone.

## arrangement
How the buttons lay out, elected by `provided("theme").dialogButtonLayout`: `"end"` (a right-aligned
row), `"stack"` (full-width buttons stacked, Cupertino-style, default on top), or `"fill"`
(equal-width buttons filling the row). **Platform structure is data, not a fork.**

## centered
Whether the title and message are centre-aligned, elected by `provided("theme").dialogTextAlign`.

## rotation
Its program's turn where it opens in a host's layer; none in the full-screen presentation.

## focusDefault()
Puts keyboard focus on the default button when the dialog opens — or the last button
when no record is marked default.

## _cb
**Internal.** The callback handed to the opening verb.

## _callCb()
**Internal.** Invokes the stored callback.

## _kidsOf()
**Internal.** The rendered button views of an arrangement, for `focusDefault`.

## presentation
`"center"` (the default): a panel centered over its context. `"full"`: the whole context,
for a viewer or a lightbox — your content edge to edge, no title or buttons unless you set
them.

## escape()
Esc picks the cancel button (`pickCancel`).

## focusFirst()
On opening, focus goes to your content's first control when it has one, else to the
default button.

## hostW
The width of the layer it paints in: its window's, or the browser window's — not the
App's, which on a page that scrolls is as tall as the page.

## hostH
The height of the layer it paints in: its window's, or the browser window's.

## _full
**Internal.** Whether it presents full-screen: `presentation == "full"`, for its own layout.

## width
The theme's `dialogWidth` (420), inside its context; the whole width full-screen.

## height
Its column's height; the whole height full-screen.

## x
Centered in its context.

## y
A third of the way down its context; the top, full-screen.

## opacity
Its open blend — it fades in and out.

## scale
It settles from slightly small as it opens — at its program's scale where it opens in a
host's layer.

## pivotX
It settles about its middle.

## pivotY
It settles about its middle.

## accessibilityLabel
The `title`, so a screen reader names the dialog as it opens.

## accessibilityHint
The `message`: a screen reader reads it after the title as the dialog opens, so "Delete
this file?" is followed by "This cannot be undone."
*/

include [ "control.declare" ]

include [ "button.declare" ]

include [ "floating.declare" ]

// a dialog button, as data

schema DialogButtonRecord [ id: string, label: string, default?: boolean, cancel?: boolean ]

class Dialog extends Floating [
    kind = "modal",
    pressPolicy = "block",
    scrollPolicy = "block",
    takesFocus = true,
    focusScope = modal,
    defaultPlacement = content,

    presentation: "center" | "full" = "center",
    title: string = "",
    message: string = "",
    accessibilityRole = dialog,
    accessibilityLabel = { title },
    accessibilityHint = { message },
    tone: string = "",
    buttons: DialogButtonRecord[]? = null,
    _cb: (id: string) -> void? = null,

    hostW: number = { this.host != null ? this.host.width : app.hostWidth > 0 ? app.hostWidth : app.width },     // a window's, else the browser window's
    hostH: number = { this.host != null ? this.host.height : app.hostHeight > 0 ? app.hostHeight : app.height },
    _full: boolean = { this.presentation == "full" },

    width = { this._full ? this.hostW : Math.min(this.hostW - 32, provided("theme").dialogWidth) },
    height = { this._full ? this.hostH : this.col.height },
    x = { (this.hostW - this.width) / 2 },
    y = { this._full ? 0 : Math.max(40, (this.hostH - this.height) / 3) },

    // motion: it fades in and settles from slightly small; full-screen, it fades

    opacity = { this.openT },
    scale = { this._full ? 1 : this._baseScale * (0.96 + 0.04 * this.openT) },
    rotation = { this._full ? 0 : this._baseRotation },
    pivotX = { this.width / 2 },
    pivotY = { this.height / 2 },

    // the delivery seam — override declaratively, or pass a callback to a verb

    picked(id: string) { },

    open(t: string, m: string, btns: array, tone_: string, cb_: (id: string) -> void?) {
        title = "" + (t || "")
        message = "" + (m || "")
        tone = "" + (tone_ || "")
        buttons = btns
        this._cb = cb_ || null
        this.show()
        },

    // ── the idioms ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    notice(t: string, m: string, cb_: (id: string) -> void?) { this.open(t, m, [({ id: "ok", label: "OK", default: true })], "", cb_) },

    error(t: string, m: string, cb_: (id: string) -> void?) { this.open(t, m, [({ id: "ok", label: "OK", default: true })], "error", cb_) },

    ask(t: string, m: string, cb_: (id: string) -> void?) {
        this.open(t, m, [
            ({ id: "cancel", label: "Cancel", cancel: true }),
            ({ id: "no", label: "No" }),
            ({ id: "yes", label: "Yes", default: true }),
        ], "", cb_)
        },

    // Dismiss-then-deliver, the same contract as Menu's pick: transient
    // chrome leaves the screen (one painted frame) before the action runs,
    // so a slow verb can never freeze the dialog in place.

    pick(id: string) {
        if (!shown) return                       // the double-activation guard
        const f = this._cb
        this.close()
        afterDelay(0, () => { this.picked(id); if (f != null) this._callCb(f, id) })
        },

    _callCb(f: (id: string) -> void, id: string) { f(id) },

    _kidsOf(b: View) -> array { return b.children },

    // the buttons are records until the opening settle closes; the default one
    // is real, placed and focusable on the far side of it (Floating calls this
    // after that settle)

    focusFirst() { this.focusDefault() },

    focusDefault() {
        if (!shown) return
        if (Focus.focusIn(this.col.content)) return   // your content's first control, when it has one
        const b = this.col.btns
        const kids = this._kidsOf(this.arrangement == "stack" ? b.stackInner : b.rowInner)
        const d = kids.find((k) => k.primary)
        if (d != null || kids.length > 0) Focus.focus(d != null ? d : kids[kids.length - 1])
        else if (!Focus.focusIn(this)) this._holdFocusItself()
        },

    pickDefault() {
        const b = (this.buttons || []).find((x) => x.default)
        if (b != null) this.pick("" + b.id)
        },

    pickCancel() {
        const b = (this.buttons || []).find((x) => x.cancel)
        if (b != null) this.pick("" + b.id)
        else this.close()
        },

    escape() { this.pickCancel() },

    // Return picks the default unless the focused view takes Return itself:
    // a focused control presses itself (Return on Cancel is Cancel), and a
    // multi-line field types a line break

    returnTaken() -> boolean {
        const f = Focus.getFocus() as any
        if (f == null || !Focus.holds(this)) return false
        return f.multiline === true || typeof f.press == "function"
        },

    enterKeys: Keys [
        onKeyDown(e: KeyEvent) {
            if (classroot.shown && classroot.isTop() && e.key == "Enter" && !classroot.returnTaken()) classroot.pickDefault()
            }
        ],

    // buttons → Button instances through the internal derived dataset. The
    // "stack" arrangement reorders: the default on top, cancel at the bottom
    // (the Cupertino stacked alert); row arrangements keep authored order.

    arrangement: string = { provided("theme").dialogButtonLayout },
    centered: boolean = { provided("theme").dialogTextAlign == "center" },

    data: Dataset [ contents = { { b: classroot.arrangement == "stack"
        ? [...(classroot.buttons || [])].sort((p, q) => (q.default ? 1 : q.cancel ? -1 : 0) - (p.default ? 1 : p.cancel ? -1 : 0))
        : classroot.buttons || [] } } ],

    // the dimming behind the panel — paint only; the dialog's `block` policy is
    // what stops presses aimed past it

    scrim: View [ pointerEvents = "none",
        x = { -classroot.x }, y = { -classroot.y },
        width = { classroot.hostW }, height = { classroot.hostH },
        fill = { provided("theme").scrimFill },
        opacity = { (provided("theme").scrimOpacity) * (classroot._full ? 0 : 1) } ],

    panel: View [ width = 100%, height = 100%,
        cornerRadius = { classroot._full ? 0 : provided("theme").dialogCornerRadius ?? provided("theme").menuCornerRadius + 2 },
        fill = { provided("theme").surface },
        stroke = { classroot._full ? null : stroke(1, provided("theme").line) },
        shadow = { classroot._full ? null : provided("theme").dialogShadow ?? provided("theme").menuShadow } ],

    // the 20pt margin is the column's own content box; the fill, the edge and
    // the shadow stay on the panel

    col: View [ width = 100%, padding = { classroot._full ? 0 : 20 },
        layout: SimpleLayout [ axis = y, spacing = 12 ],

        ttl: Text [ fontWeight = bold, wrap = true, width = 100%,
            visible = { classroot.title != "" },
            fontSize = { provided("theme").dialogTitleFontSize },
            textAlign = { classroot.centered ? "center" : "left" },
            textColor = { classroot.tone == "error" ? (provided("theme").error) : provided("theme").text },
            text = { classroot.title } ],

        msg: Text [ wrap = true, width = 100%,
            fontSize = { provided("theme").dialogBodyFontSize },
            textAlign = { classroot.centered ? "center" : "left" },
            visible = { classroot.message != "" },
            textColor = { provided("theme").textMuted }, text = { classroot.message } ],

        content: View [ width = 100%,
            height = { classroot._full ? classroot.hostH : this.contentHeight },
            layout: SimpleLayout [ axis = y, spacing = 10 ]
            ],

        btns: View [ width = 100%, visible = { (classroot.buttons || []).length > 0 },
            height = { classroot.arrangement == "stack" ? this.stackInner.height : this.rowInner.height },

            // two arrangements, one visible — layout attributes are
            // literal-only (the surface), so the axes are two containers

            rowInner: View [ visible = { classroot.arrangement != "stack" },
                height = { this.contentHeight },

                // "end": the buttons' own width — 86 each, 10 between. (Not
                // contentWidth: a button's width reads its row's in the "fill"
                // arrangement, so the row's extent leaves the buttons out.)

                x = { classroot.arrangement == "end" ? parent.width - this.width : 0 },
                width = { classroot.arrangement == "end" ? Math.max(0, (classroot.buttons || []).length * 96 - 10) : parent.width },
                layout: SimpleLayout [ axis = x, spacing = 10 ],
                datapath = { classroot.data.value },
                Button [ datapath = :b[], label = { "" + :label },
                    width = { classroot.arrangement == "fill"
                            ? (this.parent.width - 10 * (((classroot.buttons || []).length || 1) - 1)) / ((classroot.buttons || []).length || 1)
                            : 86 },
                    primary = { !!:default },
                    onClick() { classroot.pick("" + :id) }
                    ]
                ],

            stackInner: View [ visible = { classroot.arrangement == "stack" },
                height = { this.contentHeight }, width = 100%,
                layout: SimpleLayout [ axis = y, spacing = 8 ],
                datapath = { classroot.data.value },
                Button [ datapath = :b[], label = { "" + :label },
                    width = 100%,
                    primary = { !!:default },
                    onClick() { classroot.pick("" + :id) }
                    ]
                ]
            ]
        ]
    ]
